      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "60dd74dd-e899-5150-a40a-be0605adca45",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Systems: RNA polymerase III, ribosomes\n- Concepts: SINEs, ribosomal RNA, tRNA, translation, ribosomal subunits, ribosomal proteins\n\nFacts:\n- Short-interspersed nuclear elements (SINEs) are transcribed by RNA polymerase III.\n- RNA polymerase III is known to transcribe ribosomal RNA and transfer RNA.\n- Ribosomal RNA and transfer RNA are vital to ribosomal assembly and messenger RNA translation.\n- Ribosomes consist of a small ribosomal subunit and a large ribosomal subunit.\n- The small ribosomal subunit reads RNA.\n- The large ribosomal subunit joins amino acids to form a polypeptide chain.\n- Ribosomal proteins, together with ribosomal RNA, make up the ribosomal subunits involved in cellular translation.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "0b4bc1f3-a0a5-5c5e-a58a-d3d0d1961565",
        "text": "Page: Short interspersed nuclear elements (SINEs). Sentence: Short-interspersed nuclear elements are transcribed by RNA polymerase III which is known to transcribe ribosomal RNA and tRNA, two types of RNA vital to ribosomal assembly and mRNA translation.\nPage: Ribosome. Sentence: Ribosomes consist of two major components: the small ribosomal subunit, which reads the RNA, and the large subunit, which joins amino acids to form a polypeptide chain.\nPage: Ribosomal protein. Sentence: A ribosomal protein (r-protein or rProtein) is any of the proteins that, in conjunction with rRNA, make up the ribosomal subunits involved in the cellular process of translation.",
